Chapter: ProbeApproxEq

 `~` is the only equality Real has (`==` on a Real is refused, CDX2085), and
 on bare metal it is a 4-ULP band: both operands are mapped to monotonic
 ordinals and the ordinal distance is compared against 4 (X86_64.codex,
 emit-approx-eq: `cmp 4 / setcc be`). ZigEmitter emits `~` and `~0` alike as
 zig `==`, which narrows the band to zero width.

 One ULP apart is therefore the whole test: inside the band, not identical.
 0.1 + 0.2 and 0.3 are adjacent doubles -- the sum rounds to the
 representable value one step above the literal's -- so the pair is one ULP
 apart by construction, with no bit-twiddling builtins involved (the precise
 f32 version of this probe, probe-real-approx-bits.codex, is blocked on a
 missing bits-to-real-approx emitter and refuses rather than answering).
 Controls: a value against itself must answer 1 on any self-consistent arm,
 and 0.31 is far outside any band, so an arm answering the same thing
 everywhere is visibly not the language.

 A caution the truth run must settle: if the constant folder evaluates
 `~` itself at compile time, both arms inherit the compiler's own answer
 and the probe shows nothing. The defs are kept apart to discourage that;
 if the banked IR carries no IrApproxEq node, reshape the probe before
 believing either arm.

 Expected on bare metal:

   one-ulp 1
   same 1
   far 0

 A plug that emits == prints one-ulp 0.

Section: Values

  d-sum : Real
  d-sum = 0.1 + 0.2

  d-lit : Real
  d-lit = 0.3

  d-far : Real
  d-far = 0.31

Section: Main

  opening : [Console] Nothing = act
    print-line-uni ("one-ulp " & show (if d-sum ~ d-lit then 1 else 0))
    print-line-uni ("same " & show (if d-lit ~ d-lit then 1 else 0))
    print-line-uni ("far " & show (if d-sum ~ d-far then 1 else 0))
  end
